WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:08.800
&gt;&gt; So, our next speaker is
actually Maxim Grechkin,

00:00:08.810 --> 00:00:10.355
from the University
of Washington,

00:00:10.355 --> 00:00:13.320
and he's going to
talk about EZLearn.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.760
&gt;&gt; Thanks for the introduction.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:17.520
Quickly mention, this
is a joint work with

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:19.470
[inaudible] from Microsoft
Research and my advisor,

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:22.345
Bill Hall, from
University of Washington.

00:00:22.345 --> 00:00:27.785
So, the motivation for this work
comes from bio-medicine.

00:00:27.785 --> 00:00:30.470
We are looking at increasingly

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:32.780
a cheaper and cheaper
sequencing technologies,

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:34.230
that produce more and more data,

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:37.330
that is then being
pushed into repositories,

00:00:37.330 --> 00:00:39.430
like Gene Expression
Omnibus, ArrayExpress,

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:42.060
that have millions of

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:43.630
samples corresponding to

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:45.200
potentially billions
of data points,

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:52.075
and it's almost 40 terabytes
of data in ArrayExpress now.

00:00:52.075 --> 00:00:53.545
The trouble though is that,

00:00:53.545 --> 00:00:55.400
even though there are
a lot of data is there,

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:56.900
data annotation is
kind of lacking.

00:00:56.900 --> 00:00:58.530
So, this graph shows

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:01.390
exclusive growth on
submitted data samples,

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:03.965
but as you can
see on the bottom,

00:01:03.965 --> 00:01:07.140
the number of curated samples
is growing slowly,

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:08.160
because they're
doing it manually.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:13.220
So, we want to automate this
curation of scientific data.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:14.725
Specifically, gene
expression data.

00:01:14.725 --> 00:01:17.845
And the most natural
first step annotation

00:01:17.845 --> 00:01:18.920
is just a tissue sample.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:20.925
Is it a liver cell?
Is it a kidney cell?

00:01:20.925 --> 00:01:23.200
And there is
a project spearheaded

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:25.935
by Chan Zuckerberg Initiative,

00:01:25.935 --> 00:01:28.960
that tries to actually
build a Human Cell Atlas.

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:30.325
So it's a very
important problem,

00:01:30.325 --> 00:01:32.790
and we have a lot of
data collected already

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:35.370
on legacy technologies
in those repositories,

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:37.055
and we want to annotate it.

00:01:37.055 --> 00:01:39.935
Well, so you might
ask that this,

00:01:39.935 --> 00:01:42.450
we can just search, so this
repository has a Search Bar.

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:45.465
What happens if you type
things in that Search Bar?

00:01:45.465 --> 00:01:46.810
Well, it turns
out that Precision

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:47.860
and Recall are pretty low,

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:49.605
so if you search for liver,

00:01:49.605 --> 00:01:51.390
less than half of the stuff
that you get out is

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:54.830
actual liver and so on.

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:57.790
So, let's formulate the problem

00:01:57.790 --> 00:01:59.725
a little bit more concretely.

00:01:59.725 --> 00:02:00.790
So, we have a repository that

00:02:00.790 --> 00:02:02.420
contains a bunch of samples.

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:04.975
Each sample, each data item,

00:02:04.975 --> 00:02:06.730
consists of a data sample,

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:10.110
which for Gene
Expression data sets,

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:13.525
you can think of as a vector
of 20,000 real values,

00:02:13.525 --> 00:02:15.740
and there's also often
a text description.

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:17.385
So, people are uploading

00:02:17.385 --> 00:02:19.060
those data items into

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:20.590
the repository for
other peoples to use,

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:23.960
so they describe those items.

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:26.795
Those descriptions
can vary in accuracy.

00:02:26.795 --> 00:02:28.790
So sometimes, the description

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:30.245
will explicitly
mention the tissue.

00:02:30.245 --> 00:02:32.775
Sometimes, an
example on the right,

00:02:32.775 --> 00:02:34.340
it will be more cryptic,

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:36.250
it will use some abbreviations.

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:38.880
And in other cases, it
will be completely missing.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:40.745
And to point out,

00:02:40.745 --> 00:02:44.000
we now know about
5,000 different human

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.775
tissues in the predefined
Ontology in science,

00:02:47.775 --> 00:02:50.850
and more and more different
cell types are being

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:54.050
discovered every day with
advances in technology,

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:55.910
just like Single
Cell Sequencing.

00:02:55.910 --> 00:02:59.830
Also, [inaudible] I'm
going to show results of

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:01.835
our approach on
a different data set

00:03:01.835 --> 00:03:04.320
from the project,
called VizioMetrics,

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:06.420
which is a project that tries

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:08.670
to look into scientific images,

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:10.535
and tries to classify

00:03:10.535 --> 00:03:13.520
figures from scientific papers
into different types,

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:16.080
into different
visualization types.

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:19.200
The most natural thing to do is,

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:20.920
data annotation is
just a classification.

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:22.300
So, if you have a classifier,

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:26.270
you can get labels and
your problem is solved.

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:28.320
So, what's the bottleneck here?

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:30.340
Well, the bottleneck
is that you have to you

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:32.480
have to find
labeled data somewhere.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:35.280
So, you labeled
a bunch of samples

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:38.330
yourself or you somehow
got labeled data.

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:41.055
And in this case, you label
bar chart and pie chart.

00:03:41.055 --> 00:03:44.750
But then, you will inevitably

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:48.700
miss samples in the longer
[inaudible] this particular case,

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:50.390
you'll miss a phylogenetic tree,

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:55.100
maybe a picture of a platypus
or something like that.

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:57.500
And where do we get
that labeled data to begin with?

00:03:57.500 --> 00:03:59.505
You can crowdsource it but

00:03:59.505 --> 00:04:02.880
even in figure
annotation example,

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:05.740
explaining people in
the Mechanical Turk the type of

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:07.200
this visualization or type of

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:09.325
this visualization
might be complicated,

00:04:09.325 --> 00:04:11.710
and if you want to
ask them to annotate

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:13.710
thousands of different
human tissue samples,

00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:14.820
it's going to be hopeless.

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:16.380
So, you have to either hire

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:18.265
expensive experts,
do it yourself,

00:04:18.265 --> 00:04:22.140
so this kind of bottleneck
of this approach.

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:23.805
There are smarter tricks.

00:04:23.805 --> 00:04:25.760
So, there is a paper and nibs.

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:28.620
Last year, two years ago
on data programming,

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:31.410
where you write
labeling functions,

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:34.000
which produce you
noisy heuristic labels,

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:36.360
and different different
Labeling Functions

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:38.380
can produce different labels
for the same sample.

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:41.110
And then they have a framework
to produce a trading data

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:43.900
set out of those
Labeling Functions

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:45.640
or you can use
Zero-shot learning.

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:47.780
So, Zero-shot learning
approaches that allow you

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:50.340
to skip labeling
some of the classes.

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:52.000
But that kind of
assumes that you have

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:53.915
labels for other
related classes,

00:04:53.915 --> 00:04:57.210
and you know the structure
of your label space.

00:04:57.210 --> 00:04:59.260
So, what we're going to do

00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:00.600
is we're going to
take advantage of

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.900
our specific problem formulation

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:06.085
and introduce an approach
called, EZLearn.

00:05:06.085 --> 00:05:07.990
And specifically, what we

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:10.400
observed in the problem
that we're working with,

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:13.885
is presence of
this Organic Supervision.

00:05:13.885 --> 00:05:17.260
Organic Supervision, we
define it as two components.

00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:18.660
First component is a Lexicon.

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:21.110
So, each label that
we are trying to

00:05:21.110 --> 00:05:23.715
predict is not
just an abstract entity.

00:05:23.715 --> 00:05:25.835
It actually contains

00:05:25.835 --> 00:05:28.840
an English word that
represents this,

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:32.330
it's a liver, we
know the word liver.

00:05:32.330 --> 00:05:33.835
And then, in sciences,

00:05:33.835 --> 00:05:35.200
we actually have
those ontologies,

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:37.710
where we might have some
synonyms for liver not actually

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:38.850
all in synonyms but some of

00:05:38.850 --> 00:05:40.665
the synonyms for
different classes.

00:05:40.665 --> 00:05:44.115
And also, people who
produce this data,

00:05:44.115 --> 00:05:45.965
they would have
text descriptions.

00:05:45.965 --> 00:05:48.140
Sometimes, most of them,

00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:50.105
their description
will be pretty noisy.

00:05:50.105 --> 00:05:53.220
But occasionally, they would
mention an original text,

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:54.810
an original term by name.

00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:57.785
So for example, in
the figure comprehension case,

00:05:57.785 --> 00:06:01.635
this caption of
this particular image,

00:06:01.635 --> 00:06:05.965
explicitly mentions that
this is scatter plot.

00:06:05.965 --> 00:06:08.730
So, to use this information,

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:11.630
we introduce an approach
we call EZLearn,

00:06:11.630 --> 00:06:13.410
which essentially combines

00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:15.050
Distant Supervision
and Co-training.

00:06:15.050 --> 00:06:17.370
So, we use Distant Supervision,

00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:18.580
which is an idea of using this

00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:20.380
extra information
from Ontology and

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:23.980
text description to find
us initial set of labels.

00:06:23.980 --> 00:06:26.530
And then we train an auxiliary
classifier that works in

00:06:26.530 --> 00:06:29.110
a text domain and
predicts the label.

00:06:29.110 --> 00:06:31.740
And that output of that
classifier are used to train

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:36.060
the main classifier from
the original data domain.

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:41.730
So, either Gene Expression
data or figure values.

00:06:41.730 --> 00:06:44.280
Classifiers, we use
it pretty simple.

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:45.790
So, we don't want
to assume that you

00:06:45.790 --> 00:06:47.605
need to have
sophisticated classifiers.

00:06:47.605 --> 00:06:49.415
Have linear regression
for the data domain

00:06:49.415 --> 00:06:51.495
after some deep future
transformations.

00:06:51.495 --> 00:06:55.545
And then, we use fast text
for the text domain.

00:06:55.545 --> 00:06:58.370
And so, before we evaluate,

00:06:58.370 --> 00:07:03.090
let me describe the tasks
in a little finer detail.

00:07:03.090 --> 00:07:05.300
So we have over 100,000

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:06.710
Gene Expression samples in the

00:07:06.710 --> 00:07:07.890
repositories that
we're looking at.

00:07:07.890 --> 00:07:10.330
There are almost 5,000

00:07:10.330 --> 00:07:12.715
different tissue types
that want to predict.

00:07:12.715 --> 00:07:15.230
Each on average,

00:07:15.230 --> 00:07:17.580
those samples have descriptions

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:20.045
that mention three and
a half different labels.

00:07:20.045 --> 00:07:22.800
So, we have this set
of noisy labels.

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:25.320
And on the other end
of the spectrum,

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:27.815
for images, we have
over a million images.

00:07:27.815 --> 00:07:32.430
We have a growing Ontology
of figure types that we

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:34.580
are coming up with

00:07:34.580 --> 00:07:37.160
more and more types that we
want to annotate every time.

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:39.230
But then, most of
the captions don't explicitly

00:07:39.230 --> 00:07:42.600
mention the figure type.

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:44.655
And so, also for evaluation,

00:07:44.655 --> 00:07:48.120
because we have an Ontology

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:50.320
that we are trying to
predict labels from,

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:51.950
we want to take advantage of

00:07:51.950 --> 00:07:54.180
the Ontology and
evaluation step as well.

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:56.685
So, we're using Ontology-based
precision and recall,

00:07:56.685 --> 00:08:00.240
where we propagate labels
from a correct prediction

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:02.340
and sort of from a correct label

00:08:02.340 --> 00:08:03.300
and from a predictive label

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:04.430
up to the root of the Ontology.

00:08:04.430 --> 00:08:09.920
And then, measure Precision
And recall in a standard way.

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:12.330
This allows us to capture

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:14.790
differences between being almost

00:08:14.790 --> 00:08:16.435
right and being close to right,

00:08:16.435 --> 00:08:18.620
and being completely far off.

00:08:18.620 --> 00:08:23.080
And so, looking at
the tissue annotation example,

00:08:23.080 --> 00:08:24.410
we compare with
the previous state of

00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:25.830
the art approach
that was trained on

00:08:25.830 --> 00:08:30.005
15,000 manually labelled data
samples, It's called URSA.

00:08:30.005 --> 00:08:32.625
And looking at the area
under precision recall curve,

00:08:32.625 --> 00:08:36.325
we significantly outperform
this previous approach.

00:08:36.325 --> 00:08:39.120
We'll also see that
if we use coEM,

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:40.440
a variant of Co-training,

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:43.165
using their 15,000 samples,
we get improvement.

00:08:43.165 --> 00:08:44.410
So, the advantage here is that,

00:08:44.410 --> 00:08:46.840
URSA just used their
15,000 samples.

00:08:46.840 --> 00:08:49.910
A coEM would use that
15,000 label samples,

00:08:49.910 --> 00:08:53.240
and then about
100,000 of the rest of

00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:54.860
the samples that don't
have explicit labels

00:08:54.860 --> 00:08:57.140
but it would iterate
and improve the labels.

00:08:57.140 --> 00:08:59.470
I'm interested actually
if you have access

00:08:59.470 --> 00:09:00.460
to that entire repository and

00:09:00.460 --> 00:09:01.500
just do distance supervision,

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:05.080
you'd do pretty well.

00:09:05.080 --> 00:09:07.375
And then EZLearn has

00:09:07.375 --> 00:09:09.340
been a combination of
Distance Supervision,

00:09:09.340 --> 00:09:13.470
and coEM does better
than either of those,

00:09:13.470 --> 00:09:19.510
showing that both components
are important.

00:09:19.510 --> 00:09:23.940
So, those are
precision recall curves

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:26.560
for tissue annotation example

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:27.680
in the left and figure

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:29.260
comprehensions example
on the right.

00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:31.280
And blue is our method,

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:33.570
and red is previous state
of the art approach,

00:09:33.570 --> 00:09:36.110
and green dot is
what you get if you

00:09:36.110 --> 00:09:39.810
just search using
Distant Supervision.

00:09:39.810 --> 00:09:43.360
Also, we want to investigate
why exactly that happens.

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:47.680
On the right, we vary
the amount of unlabeled

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:51.520
with the amount of data
without explicit labels that

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:55.060
we feed into
either EZLearn or coEM.

00:09:55.060 --> 00:09:56.870
And we see that,

00:09:56.870 --> 00:10:01.210
even though coEM can improve
with more unlabeled data,

00:10:01.210 --> 00:10:04.440
EZLearn's improvement
is significantly higher.

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:06.490
And then, we can see that,

00:10:06.490 --> 00:10:08.380
that probably happens
because EZLearn

00:10:08.380 --> 00:10:10.520
picks up those cases from

00:10:10.520 --> 00:10:15.450
a longer tail with more more
unlabeled training data.

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:17.745
And so, to conclude,

00:10:17.745 --> 00:10:20.720
EZLearn combines Distant
Supervision and Co-training

00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:22.255
to allow you to

00:10:22.255 --> 00:10:24.710
solve a problem in
this organic supervision regime,

00:10:24.710 --> 00:10:26.880
where you have
a bunch of data that

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:29.300
was put in the
repository without

00:10:29.300 --> 00:10:30.990
a new machine or
readable labels but there are

00:10:30.990 --> 00:10:32.670
human readable labels that

00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:34.660
sometimes contain
relevant information for you.

00:10:34.660 --> 00:10:36.280
So, you don't have
to label the data,

00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:37.780
you can just do this Co-training

00:10:37.780 --> 00:10:39.245
plus Distance
Supervision approach.

00:10:39.245 --> 00:10:41.530
And it's also very useful
for cases where you

00:10:41.530 --> 00:10:45.000
have change in Ontology
or grow in Ontologies.

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:47.700
So if you want to find,

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:50.740
but you know Biologists

00:10:50.740 --> 00:10:53.790
who maintain
an Ontology of tissues,

00:10:53.790 --> 00:10:55.650
add another tissue type
in Ontology,

00:10:55.650 --> 00:10:57.280
we can just throw around
the method without actually

00:10:57.280 --> 00:10:59.890
finding manual labels for
that particular tissue.

00:10:59.890 --> 00:11:04.170
Yeah, thanks. Any questions?

